{"st":"NC","name":"North Carolina","seats":[{"census":1789,"seats":5},{"census":1790,"seats":10},{"census":1800,"seats":12},{"census":1810,"seats":13},{"census":1820,"seats":13},{"census":1830,"seats":13},{"census":1840,"seats":9},{"census":1850,"seats":8},{"census":1860,"seats":7},{"census":1870,"seats":8},{"census":1880,"seats":9},{"census":1890,"seats":9},{"census":1900,"seats":10},{"census":1910,"seats":10},{"census":1920,"seats":10},{"census":1930,"seats":11},{"census":1940,"seats":12},{"census":1950,"seats":12},{"census":1960,"seats":11},{"census":1970,"seats":11},{"census":1980,"seats":11},{"census":1990,"seats":12},{"census":2000,"seats":13},{"census":2010,"seats":13},{"census":2020,"seats":14}],"events":[{"congress":1,"year":1789,"kind":"statehood","title":"Ratified the Constitution","text":"North Carolina ratified the Constitution on November 21, 1789, the 12th state to do so. The Constitution gave it 5 seats in the House until the first census.","sources":["https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R47747.html","https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":3,"year":1792,"kind":"census","title":"1790 census apportionment","text":"After the 1790 census, North Carolina's House delegation grew from 5 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":8,"year":1802,"kind":"census","title":"1800 census apportionment","text":"After the 1800 census, North Carolina's House delegation grew from 10 to 12 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":13,"year":1812,"kind":"census","title":"1810 census apportionment","text":"After the 1810 census, North Carolina's House delegation grew from 12 to 13 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":18,"year":1822,"kind":"census","title":"1820 census apportionment","text":"After the 1820 census, North Carolina kept 13 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":23,"year":1832,"kind":"census","title":"1830 census apportionment","text":"After the 1830 census, North Carolina kept 13 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":28,"year":1842,"kind":"census","title":"1840 census apportionment","text":"After the 1840 census, North Carolina's House delegation shrank from 13 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":33,"year":1852,"kind":"census","title":"1850 census apportionment","text":"After the 1850 census, North Carolina's House delegation shrank from 9 to 8 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"census","title":"1860 census apportionment","text":"After the 1860 census, North Carolina's House delegation shrank from 8 to 7 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"census","title":"1870 census apportionment","text":"After the 1870 census, North Carolina's House delegation grew from 7 to 8 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"census","title":"1880 census apportionment","text":"After the 1880 census, North Carolina's House delegation grew from 8 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 48th Congress (1883–1885), North Carolina elected one of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"census","title":"1890 census apportionment","text":"After the 1890 census, North Carolina kept 9 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":58,"year":1902,"kind":"census","title":"1900 census apportionment","text":"After the 1900 census, North Carolina's House delegation grew from 9 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"census","title":"1910 census apportionment","text":"After the 1910 census, North Carolina kept 10 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"census","title":"1930 census apportionment","text":"After the 1930 census, North Carolina's House delegation grew from 10 to 11 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"census","title":"1940 census apportionment","text":"After the 1940 census, North Carolina's House delegation grew from 11 to 12 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":83,"year":1952,"kind":"census","title":"1950 census apportionment","text":"After the 1950 census, North Carolina kept 12 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"census","title":"1960 census apportionment","text":"After the 1960 census, North Carolina's House delegation shrank from 12 to 11 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":93,"year":1972,"kind":"census","title":"1970 census apportionment","text":"After the 1970 census, North Carolina kept 11 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"census","title":"1980 census apportionment","text":"After the 1980 census, North Carolina kept 11 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":99,"year":1986,"kind":"court","title":"Thornburg v. Gingles","text":"In a case about North Carolina legislative districts, the Supreme Court set out the test courts use to decide whether a map dilutes minority votes under Section 2 of the Voting Rights Act.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/478/30"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"census","title":"1990 census apportionment","text":"After the 1990 census, North Carolina's House delegation grew from 11 to 12 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":103,"year":1993,"kind":"court","title":"Shaw v. Reno","text":"Voters challenged the long, narrow 12th District. The Supreme Court held that a district so irregular it can be understood only as an effort to separate voters by race can be challenged under the Equal Protection Clause.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/509/630"]},{"congress":106,"year":1996,"kind":"court","title":"Shaw v. Hunt","text":"The Supreme Court held that the 12th District was a racial gerrymander not justified by the Voting Rights Act. The legislature redrew the map in 1997.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/517/899"]},{"congress":107,"year":2001,"kind":"court","title":"Easley v. Cromartie","text":"The Supreme Court upheld the redrawn 12th District, finding the evidence did not show that race rather than politics was the main reason for its shape.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/532/234"]},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"census","title":"2000 census apportionment","text":"After the 2000 census, North Carolina's House delegation grew from 12 to 13 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":113,"year":2012,"kind":"census","title":"2010 census apportionment","text":"After the 2010 census, North Carolina kept 13 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":115,"year":2016,"kind":"court","title":"Court-ordered redraw","text":"A federal court ruled in February 2016 that the 1st and 12th Districts were racial gerrymanders. The legislature drew a new map that month, used from the 2016 election.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/15-1262","https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/north-carolina/"]},{"congress":115,"year":2017,"kind":"court","title":"Cooper v. Harris","text":"The Supreme Court agreed that race predominated in drawing the 1st and 12th Districts in the 2011 map without adequate justification.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/15-1262"]},{"congress":116,"year":2019,"kind":"court","title":"Rucho v. Common Cause","text":"Voters challenged the 2016 map as a partisan gerrymander. The Supreme Court held that such claims are political questions beyond the reach of federal courts, leaving them to states and Congress.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/18-422"]},{"congress":117,"year":2019,"kind":"court","title":"State court leads to a new map","text":"A North Carolina trial court blocked the 2016 map under the state constitution in October 2019. The legislature passed a new map in November 2019, used in the 2020 election.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/north-carolina/"]},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"census","title":"2020 census apportionment","text":"After the 2020 census, North Carolina's House delegation grew from 13 to 14 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"court","title":"Harper v. Hall","text":"The North Carolina Supreme Court struck down the 2021 congressional map as a partisan gerrymander under the state constitution. A court-adopted interim map was used in the 2022 election.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/north-carolina/"]},{"congress":119,"year":2023,"kind":"court","title":"Harper v. Hall reversed","text":"On rehearing in April 2023, the North Carolina Supreme Court overruled its 2022 decision, holding that partisan gerrymandering claims cannot be decided under the state constitution. The legislature passed a new map in October 2023, used from the 2024 election.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/north-carolina/"]},{"congress":120,"year":2025,"kind":"mid-decade","title":"Mid-decade redistricting","text":"In October 2025 the General Assembly passed a revised map that moved voters between the 1st and 3rd Districts. A three-judge federal court declined to block it on November 26, 2025, and it was used in the 2026 election.","sources":["https://abc11.com/post/judges-allow-north-carolina-use-map-drawn-bid-give-republicans-another-us-house-seat/18215872/"]}],"compactness":{"c001":{"NC-1":0.358,"NC-2":0.031,"NC-3":0.21,"NC-4":0.311,"NC-5":0.178},"c002":{"NC-1":0.311,"NC-2":0.603,"NC-3":0.145,"NC-4":0.026,"NC-5":0.187},"c003":{"NC-1":0.346,"NC-2":0.363,"NC-3":0.484,"NC-4":0.485,"NC-5":0.559,"NC-6":0.181,"NC-7":0.399,"NC-8":0.046,"NC-9":0.187,"NC-10":0.058},"c004":{"NC-1":0.346,"NC-2":0.363,"NC-3":0.484,"NC-4":0.485,"NC-5":0.559,"NC-6":0.181,"NC-7":0.399,"NC-8":0.046,"NC-9":0.187,"NC-10":0.058},"c005":{"NC-1":0.346,"NC-2":0.363,"NC-3":0.484,"NC-4":0.485,"NC-5":0.559,"NC-6":0.181,"NC-7":0.399,"NC-8":0.046,"NC-9":0.187,"NC-10":0.058},"c006":{"NC-1":0.346,"NC-2":0.363,"NC-3":0.484,"NC-4":0.485,"NC-5":0.559,"NC-6":0.181,"NC-7":0.399,"NC-8":0.046,"NC-9":0.187,"NC-10":0.058},"c007":{"NC-1":0.346,"NC-2":0.363,"NC-3":0.484,"NC-4":0.485,"NC-5":0.559,"NC-6":0.181,"NC-7":0.399,"NC-8":0.046,"NC-9":0.187,"NC-10":0.058},"c008":{"NC-1":0.056,"NC-2":0.439,"NC-3":0.089,"NC-4":0.088,"NC-5":0.181,"NC-6":0.574,"NC-7":0.571,"NC-8":0.692,"NC-9":0.453,"NC-10":0.4,"NC-11":0.396,"NC-12":0.392},"c009":{"NC-1":0.056,"NC-2":0.439,"NC-3":0.089,"NC-4":0.088,"NC-5":0.181,"NC-6":0.574,"NC-7":0.571,"NC-8":0.692,"NC-9":0.453,"NC-10":0.4,"NC-11":0.396,"NC-12":0.392},"c010":{"NC-1":0.056,"NC-2":0.439,"NC-3":0.089,"NC-4":0.088,"NC-5":0.181,"NC-6":0.574,"NC-7":0.571,"NC-8":0.692,"NC-9":0.453,"NC-10":0.4,"NC-11":0.396,"NC-12":0.392},"c011":{"NC-1":0.056,"NC-2":0.439,"NC-3":0.089,"NC-4":0.088,"NC-5":0.181,"NC-6":0.574,"NC-7":0.571,"NC-8":0.692,"NC-9":0.453,"NC-10":0.4,"NC-11":0.396,"NC-12":0.392},"c012":{"NC-1":0.056,"NC-2":0.439,"NC-3":0.089,"NC-4":0.088,"NC-5":0.181,"NC-6":0.574,"N